course: dictionary
lesson: 7524
title: Practise or practice?
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Practise is a verb, a doing word. It means doing something again and again so you get better at it. «A boy in New York practised the drums for hours every day» tells us he played again and again. Practice, with a c, is the noun, the name for the doing itself: «Practice makes perfect.»
explain: In UK English the verb is spelled practise with an s, and the noun is practice with a c. In «Confident speakers still feel nervous; practising, breathing slowly and starting with a smile help», practising is a doing word, because the speakers are doing it. In US English both words are spelled practice, so the spelling can change from country to country, but the job of the word stays the same.
explain: Practise can also mean doing something often as a habit, like practise good manners, or working as a doctor or lawyer. A quick check: put «do it again and again» or «do it often» into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you need the verb practise.
fact: Practise (verb, UK) means to do something many times to get better at it.
fact: Practice (noun) is the doing itself, as in «practice makes perfect».
fact: «Practised» is the past tense of practise: «She practised the piano every day.»
fact: «A girl in Ireland gave up the piano after one hard week; a year later her friend, who practised a little every day, now played well.» Regular practice makes a difference.
fact: In US English, practice is used for the verb as well as the noun.
exercise: Which word is the verb in «Today we practise all four operations together»? | operations | Today | *practise | together
exercise: «The boy's ____ on the drums helped him get better.» Which word fills the gap? | practise | practising | *practice | practised
exercise: What does «practised the drums for hours every day» mean? | Stopped playing the drums | Hit the drums once, very hard | *Played the drums again and again to get better | Bought some new drums
test: In «Confident speakers still feel nervous; practising, breathing slowly and starting with a smile help», what kind of word is practising? | A naming word for a place | A word for a colour | *A doing word | A word that joins two sentences
test: Which sentence uses practice as a noun? | «We practise reading every night.» | *«Practice makes perfect, so keep going.» | «She will practise the trick tomorrow.» | «They practised their spelling words.»
exam: In UK English, which spelling is the verb meaning to do something again and again to get better? | practice | practices | *practise | practicing
